window.KANI_BOOTSTRAP = {"chapters": [{"id": "home", "title": "Kani Manifesto", "group": "Home", "file": "home.md", "description": "The ethos, people and practices of Kani Home."}, {"id": "framework", "title": "Programme framework", "group": "Programme Framework", "file": "framework.md", "description": "How the ethos, pillars, people and platforms fit together."}, {"id": "research", "title": "Research, Residencies & Learning", "group": "Programme Framework", "file": "research.md", "description": "Develop knowledge, creative practices, skills and new ways of working."}, {"id": "creative-experiences", "title": "Creative Experiences", "group": "Programme Framework", "file": "creative-experiences.md", "description": "Bring people together through encounters with art, culture, ideas and making."}, {"id": "commercial", "title": "Commercial Experiences & Space Hire", "group": "Programme Framework", "file": "commercial.md", "description": "Earn income through commissioned experiences, spaces and creative hospitality."}, {"id": "outreach", "title": "Outreach & Community Engagement", "group": "Programme Framework", "file": "outreach.md", "description": "Relationships, participation and continuity across the three pillars."}, {"id": "entities", "title": "Enabling entities", "group": "Governance", "file": "entities.md", "description": "The organisations that enable the work."}, {"id": "council", "title": "Council · Option 1", "group": "Governance", "file": "council.md", "description": "Seven advisory areas. One-year appointments, reviewed annually."}, {"id": "council-option-2", "title": "Council · Option 2 & comparison", "group": "Governance", "file": "council-option-2.md", "description": "A proposed participatory council alongside the original advisory model."}, {"id": "decisions", "title": "Decision rights", "group": "Governance", "file": "decisions.md", "description": "Clear ownership for direction, creative decisions and delivery."}, {"id": "year-one", "title": "Year 1 · Minimum team", "group": "Working Team", "file": "year-one.md", "description": "Three permanent hires, existing teams and defined specialist support."}, {"id": "full-structure", "title": "Full structure", "group": "Working Team", "file": "full-structure.md", "description": "Dedicated capacity as programmes and recurring work grow."}, {"id": "growth", "title": "How the team grows", "group": "Working Team", "file": "growth.md", "description": "Add people in response to sustained work and funding."}, {"id": "heart-wellness", "title": "Heart & Wellness", "group": "Heart & Wellness", "file": "heart-wellness.md", "description": "Care and belonging throughout the year. To be developed together."}, {"id": "infrastructure", "title": "Shared infrastructure & capabilities", "group": "Shared Infrastructure", "file": "infrastructure.md", "description": "Spaces, equipment and services supporting all programmes."}, {"id": "platforms", "title": "Platforms & Original Initiatives", "group": "Platforms & Original Initiatives", "file": "platforms.md", "description": "Recognisable programmes with their own identity, brief and creative lead."}, {"id": "kani-fest", "title": "Kani Fest", "group": "Platforms & Original Initiatives", "file": "kani-fest.md", "description": "A fresh curatorial proposition for each festival edition."}, {"id": "publications", "title": "Kani Publications", "group": "Platforms & Original Initiatives", "file": "publications.md", "description": "A year-round publishing programme across the three pillars."}, {"id": "marketplaces", "title": "Curated Marketplaces", "group": "Platforms & Original Initiatives", "file": "marketplaces.md", "description": "Artisans, makers, demonstrations and public exchange."}, {"id": "new-initiatives", "title": "Developing new initiatives", "group": "Platforms & Original Initiatives", "file": "new-initiatives.md", "description": "Pilot, learn and establish what deserves to continue."}, {"id": "learnings-feedback", "title": "Learnings & Feedback", "group": "Learnings & Feedback", "file": "learnings-feedback.md", "description": "Programme learning and an evolving record of feedback. Collection is ongoing."}, {"id": "open-decisions", "title": "Open decisions", "group": "Review & Editing", "file": "open-decisions.md", "description": ""}, {"id": "version-history", "title": "Version history", "group": "Review & Editing", "file": "version-history.md", "description": "A record of how the thinking develops."}, {"id": "document-plan", "title": "Document review checklist", "group": "Review & Editing", "file": "document-plan.md", "description": ""}, {"id": "editing", "title": "How to edit and share", "group": "Review & Editing", "file": "editing.md", "description": ""}], "settings": {"title": "Kani Manifesto", "repository": "bhaumikdebanshu/KaniManifesto", "branch": "main", "version": "v0.6", "stage": "Revised draft", "updated": "4 November 2026", "kicker": "An evolving manifesto"}, "content": {"home": "## Our ethos\n\n**Proposed wording for review:**\n\nKani Home is a place for creative practice, research, learning and public exchange. It brings artists, designers, architects, researchers, craftspeople, musicians, ecologists, climate practitioners, heritage specialists, technologists and communities into conversation, creating space for both sustained enquiry and shared experiences.\n\nIts work is guided by:\n\n- **Community participation:** Develop programmes with local people, practitioners and knowledge holders.\n\n- **Experimentation:** Support exploration, making, testing and work whose outcomes are not predetermined.\n\n- **Ecological responsibility:** Engage with climate, water, materials and the relationships between people and their environments.\n\n- **Exchange across disciplines:** Connect art, design, craft, music, research and technology.\n\n- **Continuity of knowledge:** Document, publish and share work so that learning continues beyond an event or residency.\n\n- **Sustainable operation:** Develop earned income and external support that sustain the place, its people and its programme.\n\n## How we work together\n\nKani brings different disciplines and forms of knowledge into shared enquiry. An artisan, technologist, researcher and local community might shape a project together, each influencing its questions, methods and outcomes. The three pillars give this work a structure while allowing projects to move between learning, public exchange and commissioned experiences.\n\n[Outreach & Community Engagement](#outreach) builds the continuing relationships that make this participation possible, from listening and co-developing ideas to supporting access and following up after a programme.\n\n[Heart & Wellness](#heart-wellness) supports belonging, team care and shared activities throughout the year.\n\n## Structure Synopsis\n\nThree pillars guide the work. A council with annually reviewed appointments contributes expertise. A continuing team turns ideas into programmes, supported by shared spaces and resources.\n\n- [Compare the two council options](#council-option-2)\n- [Meet the Option 1 advisory roles](#council)\n- [Explore the Year 1 team](#year-one)\n- [See the programme platforms](#platforms)\n\n## Working status\n\n**Status:** An evolving proposal for discussion and collective development.\n\nThis manifesto sets out a proposed direction; the [Open Decisions](#open-decisions) section identifies what still needs collective agreement.\n\nSee [Version history](#version-history) for previous editions and the reasons behind changes.\n", "framework": "## How the organisation fits together\n\n| Layer | What it explains |\n|---|---|\n| Ethos | The values that guide Kani’s choices. |\n| Three pillars | The broad kinds of activity Kani undertakes. |\n| Outreach & Community Engagement | How Kani builds relationships, listens, enables participation and sustains connections across its programmes. |\n| Advisory & Programme Council | The expertise, relationships and strategic guidance supporting the work. |\n| Permanent working team | The people responsible for continuity, development and delivery. |\n| Shared infrastructure & capabilities | The spaces, equipment and services available to every programme. |\n| Platforms & Original Initiatives | The recognisable programmes Kani develops, including Kani Fest, Kani Publications and curated marketplaces. |\n\nPlatforms bring the pillars into practice, drawing on a shared team and infrastructure. Outreach is a continuing function across the three pillars, with responsibilities assigned to the working team.\n\nThe pillar chapters describe possible activities. Year 1 priorities will be selected according to capacity, partnerships and funding.\n\n## How a project moves across the pillars\n\nThe ethos supports transdisciplinary enquiry: people with different practices and forms of knowledge shape the questions, methods and outcomes together. The pillars describe the different activities through which that enquiry can develop.\n\n**Example:** An artisan, technologist and local knowledge holders develop an AR heritage prototype during a residency. It is shared through a public walk and documented in a publication. An institution may later commission a related learning experience. The enquiry connects research and learning, public exchange and commissioned work; publishing carries its knowledge across all three.\n\nRead [How we work together on Home](#home) and the [Outreach & Community Engagement approach](#outreach).\n", "research": "Develop knowledge, creative practices, skills and new ways of working.\n\n| Activity area | Possible activities |\n|---|---|\n| Residencies & fellowships | Artist, designer and researcher residencies; interdisciplinary collaborations; supported periods of enquiry and practice development. |\n| Ecology & futures research | Water, climate, local ecologies, sustainability and speculative futures. |\n| Craft & material innovation | Artisan collaborations, material experiments, craft processes, sustainable production and documentation. |\n| AI, AR & emerging technologies | Creative AI, local-language tools, augmented-reality interpretation, interactive media, physical computing and critical enquiry into technology’s social implications. |\n| Living knowledge & archives | Oral histories, community-led archives, craft documentation and digital storytelling. |\n| Learning & skills exchange | Mentorship, student programmes, educational partnerships, field schools, workshops and hands-on research labs. |\n| Research publication & dissemination | Essays, papers, field notes, toolkits and other research outputs developed through Kani Publications. |\n\nResearch programmes can produce exhibitions, performances, publications, prototypes and public discussions. Appropriate specialists are engaged per project; emerging technology work does not initially require a permanent technology department.\n", "creative-experiences": "Bring people together through public encounters with art, culture, ideas and making.\n\n| Activity area | Possible activities |\n|---|---|\n| Kani Fest | An interdisciplinary festival with curators appointed for each edition. |\n| Exhibitions & open studios | Art, design, installations, work-in-progress presentations and residency outcomes. |\n| Music, performance & poetry | Baithaks, concerts, theatre, readings, experimental sound and audiovisual collaborations. |\n| Film & public conversations | Screenings, filmmaker discussions, talks, panels and debates. |\n| Workshops & heritage walks | Participatory making, demonstrations and walks developed with local knowledge holders. |\n| Curated marketplaces | Selected artisans and makers, themed editions, demonstrations, workshops and associated cultural programming. |\n| Publication encounters | Book launches, readings, editorial conversations and exhibitions connected to Kani Publications. |\n\nTicketed public events and curated marketplaces remain within Creative Experiences. Charging for participation does not automatically move a programme into the commercial pillar.\n", "commercial": "Generate earned income through hospitality, spaces and commissioned experiences.\n\n| Activity area | Possible activities |\n|---|---|\n| Corporate retreats & offsites | Residential or day programmes combining meetings, stays and creative experiences. |\n| Commissioned experiences | Tailored craft, culture and creative-learning programmes for clients and institutions. |\n| Gallery & event-space hire | Client exhibitions, presentations and private events. |\n| Screening & AV-room hire | Private screenings, presentations and facilitated sessions. |\n| Office & meeting-space hire | Workspace, conference rooms and short-term team bookings. |\n| Creative hospitality & stays | Guest stays, catering and residential programme packages tied to creative, cultural or learning experiences. |\n| Commissioned publications | Partner-supported or commissioned editions developed with Kani Publications under an agreed editorial brief. |\n\nEach commercial engagement has an agreed scope, price, delivery owner and allocation of space and staff. Public-interest programming and commercial bookings share a coordinated calendar.\n", "outreach": "## Purpose\n\nOutreach is a continuing responsibility across all three pillars. It builds relationships, helps Kani understand what people need, and creates ways for people to participate in shaping its work. Communications helps people hear about a programme; outreach helps them find a way into it and influence it.\n\n## Relationships and responsibilities\n\n| Area | What we do | Who leads |\n|---|---|---|\n| Local relationships | Meet neighbourhood groups, craftspeople, educators and cultural practitioners; listen to their interests and develop activities together. | Programme team; Programme & Operations Coordinator in Year 1. |\n| Participation and access | Reach potential residents and participants; explain opportunities; identify barriers such as language, cost, timing or accessibility. | Named programme owner, supported by communications and outreach coordination. |\n| Audience development | Build relationships with groups likely to attend, invite them personally and encourage repeat participation beyond one event. | Design & Communications, alongside programme owners. |\n| Institutional connections | Develop exchanges and joint programmes with schools, colleges, cultural organisations and research institutions. | Director and relevant programme owner, with the partnerships lead where needed. |\n| Funding and commercial relationships | Approach funders, sponsors, offsite clients and venue users with relevant proposals. | Fundraising & Partnerships / Commercial & Bookings. |\n\n## Four working commitments\n\n1. **A named owner.** Every programme identifies who handles outreach and maintains the relationships afterwards. Programme owners remain responsible for commitments made to participants.\n2. **Time and budget.** Outreach begins while a programme is being developed. Budgets allow for visits, translation, travel and participation support where needed.\n3. **A shared relationship record.** Record conversations, interests, commitments and follow-ups, with consent for ongoing contact. Contact details are held in an appropriate internal record, not this public document.\n4. **A feedback loop.** Ask what worked, who was missing and what should change. Review repeat participation, collaborations and participant experience alongside attendance and communication reach.\n\n## Year 1 and growth\n\nThe **Director of Creative Practice** is accountable for the overall approach and supports key relationships. The **Programme & Operations Coordinator** coordinates local relationships, participation support and follow-up. The **Design & Communications Associate** leads audience communication with programme inputs. The retained fundraising lead handles funding relationships and relevant institutional partnerships.\n\nAllocate working time for these responsibilities. A busy festival, residency or marketplace must budget for additional outreach support when the permanent team cannot carry it.\n\nAs recurring work and funding justify it, a **Community Engagement & Outreach Coordinator** reports to the Director and works across the pillars. This role maintains continuity between programmes; programme owners still own their briefs, budgets and commitments.\n\n## Across the platforms\n\n- **Kani Fest:** Co-develop participation with local groups, plan invitations and access, and maintain relationships after the edition. Work with the Volunteer Coordinator on continuing participation; that coordinator owns induction, rota and welfare, while assigned venue or task leads direct each shift. Sustained year-round responsibilities need a scoped, funded role.\n- **Kani Publications:** Invite contributors and community knowledge holders, agree credit and permissions, and plan how publications reach their intended readers.\n- **Curated Marketplaces:** Build sustained artisan and maker relationships, support applications and participation, and gather feedback after each edition. Curatorial selection remains with the Marketplace Curator.\n\nProgramme reviews record what was heard, what changed in response and who will follow up. See [Decision rights](#decisions) for the boundaries between outreach, creative selection and financial approval.\n\n## Care within the team\n\nOutreach builds continuing relationships with communities and participants. The shared Heart & Wellness responsibility supports the people doing the work across Kani through welcome, check-ins, morale and practical support. The two work together where community members become collaborators or volunteers; volunteer rota and shift responsibilities stay with the assigned festival leads.\n\nThe [Heart & Wellness](#heart-wellness) section will be developed together; its activity programme remains open.\n", "entities": "The entities enable the work; the pillars describe activities. These are proposed operating remits, to be aligned with the entities’ actual mandates and approvals.\n\n| Entity | Proposed contribution |\n|---|---|\n| Seed of Unity Foundation — SoUF | Community, education, supported research, fellowships and public-interest programmes. |\n| Kashmir Innovation Labs — KiLab | Craft, material research, documentation, innovation and related collaborations. |\n| M/s Kani Home | Commercial experiences, space hire, hospitality and accommodation. |\n\nEvery project identifies its lead entity, budget and authorised signatory. Operational coordination under the Director does not replace the financial and contractual authority of the relevant entity.\n", "council": "**Option 1 — existing proposal, retained for comparison.** Read [Option 2 and the side-by-side comparison](#council-option-2) before choosing a model. Neither option is adopted.\n\n## Purpose\n\nThe council helps establish annual direction and priorities, contributes expertise, mentors practices and opens relationships. Senior advisors support the working team through key conversations and defined commitments. Day-to-day execution rests with staff and commissioned collaborators.\n\n## Seven advisory areas\n\nThe areas describe expertise, not named individuals or separate departments. Each area may have one or two members, depending on the expertise and support needed. Where two members share an area, they agree how to divide responsibilities and coordinate their contribution. Seven advisory areas can therefore have between seven and fourteen members when all areas are filled.\n\n| Advisory role | Main contribution |\n|---|---|\n| Art & Cultural Practice | Visual arts, film, exhibitions, artistic development and cultural relationships. |\n| Design, Systems & Technology | Programme formats, organisational systems, experiences, shared tools and infrastructure, and emerging technologies. |\n| Research, Learning & Publishing | Research direction, academic relationships, educational programmes and editorial standards. |\n| Sound & Music | Music programming, sound practices, practitioner networks and interdisciplinary collaborations. |\n| Craft, Heritage & Community | Artisan relationships, local knowledge, heritage, craft innovation and community participation. |\n| Business & Enterprise | Commercial models, pricing, retreats, bookings and market relationships. |\n| Fundraising & Resource Development | Grants, CSR relationships, sponsorships, philanthropy and institutional funding. |\n\nInstitutional partnerships are a shared responsibility across the council. Membership should include at least two people deeply connected to Kashmir’s communities and practices. Invite additional specialists for particular enquiries rather than expanding the standing council for every discipline.\n\n## Appointments and accountability\n\n- Council members serve one-year terms. Membership is reviewed annually, and appointments may be renewed.\n\n- The annual review considers contribution, agreed commitments, continuity and the expertise needed for the next year.\n\n- One member serves as Convenor for one year, coordinating agendas and follow-up without an additional artistic veto.\n\n- Each member has a written remit, agreed time commitment and compensation arrangement.\n\n- Each member undertakes a small set of concrete commitments, such as introducing partners, mentoring a project or helping develop a programme.\n\n- Departing members document commitments and hand over relationships to the working team.\n\n## Working method and disagreements\n\nHold quarterly planning and review meetings, with shorter project conversations as needed. The Director attends; relevant staff and platform leads join for their items. The Programme & Operations Coordinator initially provides light secretariat support.\n\nBring concrete proposals stating purpose, audience, owner, cost, funding source and demands on space and staff. Discuss them against the mission, local relevance, creative or learning value, feasibility and what they would displace.\n\n**Voting proposal for collective review:** Seek agreement before voting. Each advisory area has one vote, whether it has one or two members. Members sharing an area agree their position; if they cannot agree, that area abstains. A quorum requires at least five advisory areas represented, with at least four affirmative area votes for a council-level decision. Conflicted members declare their interest and step out. Confirm how vacancies, shared-area conflicts and recusals affect representation before adopting this procedure; if a valid quorum cannot be established, defer the decision. This procedure remains an open decision for the group.\n\nRecord decisions, reasons, owners and next actions. Routine programme delivery proceeds within delegated authority and does not await council votes.\n", "council-option-2": "## Two options for discussion\n\n**Status: Proposed alternative — not adopted.** Option 2 sits alongside [Option 1 — Advisory & Programme Council](#council). Neither option is agreed governance. Option numbers describe alternative models; they are separate from the manifesto’s edition number.\n\n## Compare the options\n\n| Question | Option 1 — Advisory & Programme Council | Option 2 — Participatory Council |\n|---|---|---|\n| Membership | Seven advisory areas, with one or two members per area: 7–14 members when filled. | Five advisors, five ex officio programme members, two community representatives and two youth representatives: 14 seats when filled. |\n| Expertise | Seven areas spanning art, design and technology, research and publishing, music, craft and community, business and fundraising. | Five broader advisory areas build long-term capability; serving curators bring current programme experience. |\n| Curators | Relevant platform leads join for their items; no automatic council seats are specified. | Festival Curator and four track curators hold additional seats during their appointments. |\n| Community | At least two members deeply connected to Kashmir’s communities and practices; no dedicated community-selected seats are specified. | Two dedicated community seats, with selection and reporting-back arrangements developed with local participants. |\n| Youth | No dedicated youth seats are specified. | Two dedicated youth seats, selected through an accessible youth-led process. |\n| Director | Attends and leads the working team within delegated authority. | Attends as operational lead, outside the proposed 14 seats, connecting shared priorities to delivery. |\n| Terms | One-year appointments, reviewed annually and renewable. | Advisors and representatives serve one-year terms; ex officio membership follows curatorial appointments. Review the model after one year. |\n| Decision procedure | Proposed one vote per advisory area, quorum of five areas and four affirmative area votes; still open for agreement. | Decision scope, voting, quorum, vacancies and recusals must be agreed before operation. The Option 1 formula does not carry over. |\n| Working rhythm | Quarterly planning and review, defined advisory commitments and project conversations. | Quarterly council meeting preceded by an open community gathering; defined contributions and temporary working groups as needed. |\n| Main value | Concentrated expertise, mentoring and relationships through a simpler membership structure. | Direct participation by current practitioners, community members and young people. |\n| Main demand | Deliberate routes are needed for community and youth priorities to influence the council. | More coordination and participation support; professional members must make room for community and youth influence. |\n\n[Read Option 1 in full](#council). Existing [Decision rights](#decisions) remain a reference draft while the alternatives are discussed.\n\n## Heart — the shared foundation\n\nEverything we build begins with heart and grace. We approach people, places and practices with care, attention and respect. This shapes how we welcome, listen, collaborate, disagree and support one another.\n\nThis proposed foundation sits beneath all three pillars. [Heart & Wellness](#heart-wellness) can also develop year-round mindfulness, reflection and wellbeing activities across the pillars, alongside care for the working team. Programme details remain open for collective development.\n\n## Proposed council composition\n\nFour perspectives come together in one council. The groups describe contributions, not separate departments or ranks. Members can contribute beyond their category.\n\n| Group | Seats | Proposed roles and contribution |\n|---|---|---|\n| Practice & Systems Advisors | 5 | One each for Arts, Craft & Cultural Practice; Research, Learning & Publishing; Design, Systems & Technology; Business & Enterprise; Fundraising & Partnerships. Build capability, mentor the team and develop practitioner, institutional, industry and funding relationships. |\n| Programme members — ex officio | 5 | Festival Curator; Arts & New Media Curator; Sound & Music Curator; Film & Moving Image Curator; Craft & Material Practices Curator. Bring current practice, practitioner relationships and programme experience. |\n| Community representatives | 2 | Selected through a process developed with local residents, practitioners and participants. Bring priorities, access needs and lived experience; gather views and report back. |\n| Youth representatives | 2 | Young people selected through an accessible youth-led nomination or selection process. Help shape opportunities, learning and Kani’s future direction. |\n| **Total council seats** | **14** | The Director attends as operational lead, additional to these seats. Other working-team members join relevant items. |\n\nStart with one advisor per area. Invite further specialists for particular enquiries rather than automatically adding standing seats. Community and youth seats are distinct places; one person does not fill two seats.\n\n## Advisors and curators\n\nAn advisor supports the wider organisation across programmes and years. A curator develops a particular programme within an agreed brief. For example, the Arts, Craft & Cultural Practice Advisor helps build mentoring and institutional relationships; the Arts & New Media Curator develops the festival track.\n\nEx officio means membership by virtue of holding a role. Each serving curator joins through their appointment and leaves that seat when the appointment ends. These seats are additional to the advisors. An unfilled curatorial role leaves a vacant seat; no appointment is made solely to fill the council.\n\nAdvisors and community/youth representatives serve one-year terms, reviewed annually and renewable. Each has an agreed remit, time commitment and participation or compensation arrangement. Curatorial appointments include time for council work and handover.\n\nMembers step out of discussions concerning their own appointment, fees or performance. Interests in commissions, selections or partnerships are declared and handled through an agreed conflict process.\n\n## Council remit and working authority\n\nThe council collectively shapes annual priorities, the balance between programmes, participation and access, and how Kani’s work reflects its ethos. It reviews experience and recommends what to continue, change or stop. It advises on major partnerships, new platforms and long-term development.\n\nBefore adopting the model, specify which matters the council decides and which are recommendations requiring approval elsewhere. The council’s title alone creates no financial, contractual or employment authority.\n\n| Responsibility | Proposed owner |\n|---|---|\n| Shared direction, participation priorities and programme learning | Council, informed by community gatherings and the team; exact decision authority to be agreed. |\n| Realistic programme, staffing and resource plans; everyday coordination | Director within delegated authority, reporting progress and difficulties to the council. |\n| Artistic and editorial choices, schedules and delivery within approved briefs | Appointed curators, Producer, editor and relevant programme leads. Routine work does not await council approval. |\n| Budgets, contracts and delegated authority | Authorised representatives of the relevant entity. |\n| Appointment and review of the Director | An arrangement to be agreed between the enabling entities, with a named accountable route. |\n\nIndustry introductions and sponsorship support are valued alongside mentoring, local knowledge and care. The working team develops proposals and follows up on relationships. Membership does not guarantee sponsorship or confer control over programme selections.\n\n## Participation beyond the seats\n\nHold a quarterly open community gathering before the council meeting. Community and youth representatives bring its priorities to the council and report back on responses. Offer ways to contribute without attending a meeting. One person is not expected to represent every local perspective.\n\nCommunity and youth members help prepare agendas, receive the same information and have supported opportunities to influence recommendations. Budget for honoraria, travel and access needs; agree accessible languages and meeting times.\n\nWhen resources allow, pilot a small community-directed programme fund. Agree the amount, eligible activities, decision process and practical boundaries in advance. Participants help choose activities; the team supports delivery; the next gathering reviews the experience. This is a proposal, not a funded commitment.\n\n## A light working rhythm\n\n- Appoint one Convenor annually from the council. Prepare agendas with the Director and community/youth representatives, support balanced participation and follow up on actions.\n- Meet as a full council quarterly. Allocate staff time for light secretariat support.\n- Advisors agree practical contributions between meetings, such as mentoring, introductions, proposal review or developing a system with the team.\n- Form small working groups for defined questions, with relevant community and youth participation, an owner and an end date.\n- Record recommendations or decisions, reasons, action owners and review dates. Report back while keeping personal or sensitive concerns out of public records.\n\n## Before choosing an option\n\n- [ ] Discuss which model fits Kani’s purpose, capacity and participation budget.\n- [ ] Confirm seats and whether festival representation adequately connects to year-round research, learning, publishing and other programmes.\n- [ ] Develop community and youth selection, eligibility, renewal and reporting-back arrangements with the people involved.\n- [ ] Confirm authority, relationships with the enabling entities and accountability for the Director.\n- [ ] Agree decision-making, quorum, voting if used, vacancies, recusals and the Director’s participation rights. Protect meaningful community and youth influence.\n- [ ] Allocate convening, administration, honoraria and access support before appointments.\n- [ ] Review the model after one year: who participated, whose proposals shaped decisions, what was delivered and what coordination cost.\n\nChoosing Option 2 would require coordinated changes to council, decision rights and related team and programme pages. Until then, retain both options as proposals for comparison.\n", "decisions": "**Reference draft for Option 1.** [Option 2](#council-option-2) proposes an alternative council composition and remit. These decision rights have not been replaced; revise them together once a model is agreed.\n\n| Decision | Responsible role |\n|---|---|\n| Overall direction and annual priorities | Council collectively, informed by the Director and working team. |\n| Year-round programme, staff allocation and operational commitments | Director of Creative Practice, within delegated authority. |\n| Festival artistic selections | Festival Curator, informed by track curators/fellows, within the agreed brief and budget. |\n| Festival programme and resource review | Festival Curator and Producer jointly review the programme; the Curator owns artistic choices and the Producer owns delivery planning. Escalate unresolved resource conflicts to the Director. |\n| Festival confirmed timetable | Programme Coordinator maintains the schedule and change log after review by the relevant leads. |\n| Festival equipment allocation and changeovers | Technical & Infrastructure Lead under the Producer, working with venue/session leads and shared technical staff. |\n| Festival volunteer coordination | Volunteer Coordinator owns induction, rota and support; one assigned venue or task lead directs each shift. |\n| Publication editorial development and quality | Editor / Publishing Lead, within the agreed publishing brief and budget. |\n| Marketplace theme and participant selection | Marketplace Curator, within the agreed programme brief. |\n| Outreach strategy and key institutional relationships | Director, informed by programme owners and community feedback. |\n| Community relationships, participation support and follow-up | Programme & Operations Coordinator in Year 1; dedicated outreach coordinator when appointed, working with the named programme owner. |\n| Audience communication and development | Design & Communications, alongside programme owners and outreach coordination. |\n| Project schedules, participant coordination and delivery follow-up | Named programme manager or coordinator. |\n| Technical feasibility, venue readiness and production methods | Operations / Production Lead or the coordinator with appointed technical specialists. |\n| Funding proposals and funder relationships | Fundraising lead/manager with Director, programme and accounts inputs. |\n| Commercial proposals and bookings | Named commercial owner within approved pricing and booking rules. |\n| Team care, morale and working culture | Director accountable; named Heart & Wellness coordinator supports check-ins and practical care. Every lead remains responsible for their team’s working conditions. |\n| Contracts and financial commitments | Authorised representatives of the relevant entity. |\n\nWhen scope exceeds agreed resources, the relevant creative lead and Director revise the proposal or seek additional support. Council endorsement alone does not authorise spending.\n\nEvery project has one named owner and a clear budget, including when it connects pillars or platforms. For shared projects, agree who owns each deliverable and how budgets and handovers are coordinated.\n\nThe council’s proposed voting method gives one vote to each advisory area, regardless of whether it has one or two members. It remains subject to collective agreement; see [Council appointments and decision procedure](#council) and [Open Decisions](#open-decisions).\n", "year-one": "Begin with a small permanent team, supported by existing staff and funded specialist appointments. Add continuing roles as programmes, workload and resources grow.\n\nStart with **three permanent hires**, supported by the existing accounts and hospitality teams and a defined budget for retained and project expertise.\n\n## Director of Creative Practice\n\nThe single operational head of the working team.\n\n- Leads the year-round programme across the three pillars.\n\n- Is accountable for outreach and community engagement, supporting key relationships and reviewing participation and access.\n\n- Works with the council on priorities, partnerships and institutional development.\n\n- Holds the overall calendar, resource plan and programme budget within delegated authority.\n\n- Commissions curators, editors, mentors and other specialists.\n\n- Leads key fundraising relationships with retained support.\n\n- Develops and reviews platforms and original initiatives.\n\n## Roles reporting to the Director\n\n| Role | Initial responsibilities |\n|---|---|\n| Programme & Operations Coordinator | Shared calendar, resident and artist liaison, local community relationships, participation support and follow-up; production logistics, vendor coordination, commercial enquiries and booking follow-up; coordination with accounts and hospitality. |\n| Design & Communications Associate | Graphics, social media, programme announcements, audience communication and development with programme owners, presentations and documentation coordination. An early-career role with independent working ability. |\n| Existing Accounts Team | Invoicing, payments, records, expenditure tracking and financial reporting inputs. Entity approval powers remain separate. |\n| Existing Hospitality Team | Accommodation, guest arrivals, room readiness, catering arrangements, routine venue visits and visitor support. |\n\nThe Director is one of the three new permanent hires. Existing accounts and hospitality capacity is additional to those three hires.\n\n## Retained, rotating and project basis\n\n| Support role | Starting arrangement |\n|---|---|\n| Fundraising & Partnerships Lead | Part-time retainer with defined deliverables, reporting to the Director. Develops grants, CSR opportunities, sponsors, donors and institutional relationships. |\n| Proposal & Grant Writer | Commissioned for selected applications and reports; coordinated by the fundraising lead. Can be the same person where skills and capacity allow. |\n| AV Technician | Scheduled event support and maintenance visits. Train an existing staff member for basic playback and equipment checks. |\n| Student Design Intern | Paid, supervised placement under the Design & Communications Associate, with defined learning outcomes. |\n| Senior Design Mentor | Occasional support for visual direction, review and development of the associate. |\n| Festival Curator, curatorial fellows & Festival Producer | Edition-funded appointments; proposed 6–9 month paid track fellowships with mentoring and production support. |\n| Festival delivery roles | Programme Coordinator, Technical & Infrastructure Lead, venue/session leads, Artist & Guest Liaison and Volunteer Coordinator; combine roles only where capacity allows. |\n| Editor / Publishing Lead | Retained or appointed per publication or series, supported by commissioned editorial specialists. |\n| Marketplace Curator | Contract across a series of editions, supported by the permanent coordinator. |\n| Residency mentors & facilitators | Appointments against each programme’s budget, including researchers, artists, educators, creative technologists and local knowledge holders. |\n| Specialist production partners | Sound, lighting, fabrication, installation, photography, video and event crew as required. |\n\nStart with a manageable calendar. A festival or publication deadline must carry its additional staffing cost rather than simply being added to the coordinator’s existing workload.\n\n## Outreach capacity\n\nThe coordinator maintains the shared relationship record and follows up on programme feedback, while the Director supports key relationships. Allocate time for this work and fund additional outreach support during busy programmes. There is no additional permanent outreach hire in the initial three-person team. See [Outreach & Community Engagement](#outreach).\n\nKani Fest budgets for its own delivery capacity and supported volunteer teams. The permanent coordinator holds continuity; returning volunteers can take bounded team roles, while sustained responsibilities require a funded appointment. See the [festival working map](#kani-fest).\n\n## Heart & Wellness\n\nName a coordinator for team welcome, check-ins, morale, recognition and practical support, with time allocated within the starting structure. The Director is accountable for making this possible; every lead owns respectful working and realistic workloads. At festival peaks, agree additional support alongside the Producer and Volunteer Coordinator. Provide an alternative contact for concerns involving the usual lead.\n\nThe [Heart & Wellness](#heart-wellness) section will be developed together; its activity programme remains open.\n", "full-structure": "| Area | Role | Core responsibility |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Programme management | Programme Manager — Research, Residencies & Learning | Owns residencies, fellowships, research, educational relationships and participant support. |\n| Programme management | Programme Manager — Creative Experiences | Owns the public calendar and delivery coordination for exhibitions, music, film, workshops, walks, marketplaces and Kani Fest. |\n| Community engagement | Community Engagement & Outreach Coordinator | Maintains local relationships, coordinates participation and access, supports programme outreach and follows up on feedback across the pillars. |\n| Fundraising, partnerships and commercial development | Fundraising & Partnerships Manager | Owns the funding strategy and pipeline, funder relationships, grants, CSR partnerships, sponsorships, donors and institutional collaborations. |\n| Fundraising, partnerships and commercial development | Commercial & Bookings Executive | Manages enquiries, proposals, quotations, site visits, follow-up, confirmed bookings and client relationships. |\n| Fundraising, partnerships and commercial development | Proposal & Grant Writer | Develops applications, concept notes, CSR and sponsorship proposals, pitch material and narrative reports. |\n| Operations and technical delivery | Operations / Production Lead | Owns venue readiness, the master venue calendar, logistics, maintenance coordination, vendors and on-site delivery. |\n| Operations and technical delivery | AV & Technical Operator | Operates sound, projection and screening systems, maintains equipment and coordinates with specialist vendors. |\n| Design and communications | Design & Communications Lead | Owns visual communication, social media planning, audience outreach, announcements, newsletters, presentations and signage across all programmes. |\n| Design and communications | Student Design Intern | Supports production through a supervised rotating placement. |\n| Existing support teams | Accounts Team | Supports project owners and fundraising staff while retaining entity-specific approval procedures. |\n| Existing support teams | Hospitality Team | Works with production, programme and bookings staff on delivery. |\n\nAll continuing roles sit within one permanent working team led by the **Director of Creative Practice**. This is a growth model; appointments follow recurring need and funding rather than being simultaneous Year 1 hires.\n\n## Programme management\n\n- **Programme Manager — Research, Residencies & Learning:** Owns residencies, fellowships, research, educational relationships and participant support. Shapes the research and learning pipeline, from developing opportunities and selecting participants to coordinating mentors, partners and outcomes. Ensures participants receive clear support and that learning is documented and shared.\n\n- **Programme Manager — Creative Experiences:** Owns the public calendar and coordinates delivery for exhibitions, music, film, workshops, walks, marketplaces and Kani Fest. Works with the Programme Manager — Research, Residencies & Learning when projects produce public events, ensuring that each project has one named owner and clear delivery responsibilities.\n\nBoth report to the Director. Where a residency produces a public event, the two managers agree the handover and responsibilities for each part.\n\n## Community engagement and outreach\n\n**Community Engagement & Outreach Coordinator — reports to the Director**\n\nMaintains local and community relationships across programmes, coordinates participation and access support, keeps the shared relationship record and follows up on feedback. Works with programme managers on co-development and with Design & Communications on audience development. Programme owners retain responsibility for creative briefs, budgets, selections and commitments.\n\nIn Year 1 these duties are allocated to the Programme & Operations Coordinator with Director support. Establish the dedicated role when recurring relationships and participation work exceed that capacity and funding can sustain it. See [Outreach & Community Engagement](#outreach).\n\n## Fundraising, partnerships and commercial development\n\n**Fundraising & Partnerships Manager — reports to the Director**\n\nThis operational role works alongside the Advisory & Programme Council’s **Fundraising & Resource Development** advisory role. The advisor provides strategic guidance and relationships; the Manager owns day-to-day fundraising delivery, partnerships and reporting.\n\nOwns the funding strategy and pipeline, funder relationships, grants, CSR partnerships, sponsorships, donors and institutional collaborations. Oversees commercial development and coordinates funder reporting.\n\nReporting to this manager:\n\n- **Commercial & Bookings Executive:** Enquiries, proposals, quotations, site visits, follow-up, confirmed bookings and client relationships.\n\n- **Proposal & Grant Writer:** Applications, concept notes, CSR and sponsorship proposals, pitch material and narrative reports. Can remain external or retained.\n\nProgramme managers design and deliver funded activities and collect outcome evidence. Accounts supplies expenditure records and financial reporting. The Fundraising Manager coordinates the combined report and funder relationship.\n\n## Operations and technical delivery\n\n**Operations / Production Lead — reports to the Director**\n\nOwns venue readiness, the master venue calendar, logistics, maintenance coordination, vendors and on-site delivery.\n\n- **AV & Technical Operator — reports to the Operations Lead:** Operates sound, projection and screening systems, maintains equipment and coordinates with specialist vendors.\n\n- External production partners provide additional capacity per project.\n\n## Design and communications\n\n**Design & Communications Lead — reports to the Director**\n\nOwns visual communication, social media planning, audience outreach, announcements, newsletters, presentations and signage across all programmes.\n\n- **Student Design Intern — reports to the Design Lead:** Supports production through a supervised rotating placement.\n\n- Programme owners provide accurate information; the communications lead coordinates public-facing editing and approval.\n\n- Editors own publication manuscripts and editorial quality. Publication design is planned with the design team.\n\n- Photography, recording and editing receive allocated time or commissioned support.\n\n## Existing support teams\n\n- **Accounts Team:** Operationally coordinated under the Director; works with project owners and fundraising staff while retaining entity-specific approval procedures.\n\n- **Hospitality Team:** Operationally coordinated under the Director; works with production, programme and bookings staff on delivery.\n\n## Kani Fest project team\n\nThe Festival Curator and Festival Producer work alongside one another under the Director. Proposed 6–9 month paid curatorial fellows develop the tracks; the Producer coordinates programme scheduling, technical infrastructure, venue/session leads, artist liaison and volunteer coordination. These are edition appointments or allocated responsibilities, not automatic additions to the permanent team. The permanent coordinator in Year 1, or Creative Experiences Programme Manager at scale, holds continuity between editions. See the [festival working map and role explainers](#kani-fest).\n\n## Heart & Wellness\n\nA shared function with a named coordinator accountable to the Director, supporting staff, fellows, project teams and volunteers across Kani. It coordinates welcome, check-ins, recognition, listening and practical care. It can be allocated within an existing role initially; dedicated capacity follows workload and funding. All team leads retain responsibility for respectful working, realistic workloads and follow-through on concerns. See [Shared Infrastructure](#infrastructure).\n\nThe [Heart & Wellness](#heart-wellness) section will be developed together; its activity programme remains open.\n", "growth": "Growth follows recurring work and funding rather than a fixed hiring sequence.\n\n| Trigger | Staffing response |\n|---|---|\n| Credible funding opportunities require sustained follow-up. | Make Fundraising & Partnerships Manager permanent. |\n| Residencies, public programmes and commercial hires regularly overlap. | Separate programme management from operations/production coordination. |\n| Community relationships, participation support and follow-up become sustained work beyond the programme coordinator’s capacity. | Add a Community Engagement & Outreach Coordinator reporting to the Director, with recurring funding and a defined cross-programme remit. |\n| Technical activity becomes frequent and predictable. | Bring in a permanent AV & Technical Operator. |\n| Commercial enquiries and bookings become a substantial recurring business. | Add a Commercial & Bookings Executive under Fundraising & Partnerships. |\n| Research and public programmes each sustain a funded calendar. | Create dedicated managers for Research, Residencies & Learning and Creative Experiences. |\n| Publication work becomes a sustained, funded annual programme. | Expand the editor’s retained time; consider a continuing publishing role only when justified. |\n| Festival or marketplace peaks exceed normal capacity. | Add temporary producers, coordinators and crew through the relevant programme budget. |\n\nBefore each permanent addition, confirm recurring need, a clear reporting line and visibility of funding for the role’s full cost over the following 12 months. This is a proposed planning rule, not a commitment to hire.\n\nFor Kani Fest, returning volunteers may take defined team-captain responsibilities with training and staff support. Sustained work across the year should become a scoped, funded associate or fellowship opportunity where justified. This is an optional pathway based on interest, readiness and available roles, not a guaranteed promotion. See [Kani Fest](#kani-fest).\n\nReview capacity for Heart & Wellness as staff, fellows, volunteers and project teams grow. Begin with a named coordinator and allocated time; add dedicated support when recurring need and funding justify it.\n\nThe [Heart & Wellness](#heart-wellness) section will be developed together; its activity programme remains open.\n", "heart-wellness": "## Heart & Wellness\n\nA space for care, belonging and wellbeing throughout the year, supporting the people who work, learn and participate at Kani.\n\n**To be developed together.** We will populate this section with its approach, activities and working arrangements as the conversation develops.\n", "infrastructure": "This layer shows what every programme can draw on. It does not create additional posts or duplicate the permanent team.\n\n| Shared capability | Resources and services | Responsible roles |\n|---|---|---|\n| Spaces | Level 1 gallery with track lights and four movable walls; Level 2 screening/AV room; Level 3 office and meeting facilities; guest house accommodating up to 15 people. | Programme & Operations Coordinator initially; Operations Lead as the team grows; hospitality for stays. |\n| Technical infrastructure | Sound, projection, lighting, connectivity, equipment operation and upkeep. | Scheduled AV technician initially; permanent AV Operator when justified. |\n| Production | Installation, fabrication coordination, event logistics, procurement and specialist vendors. | Coordinator initially; Operations / Production Lead at scale. |\n| Design, communications & audience support | Graphics, social media, publicity, registrations, mailing lists, signage and audience feedback. | Design & Communications role with programme inputs. |\n| Community relationships & participation support | Shared relationship records, consent for ongoing contact, access planning, partner follow-up and feedback learning. | Programme & Operations Coordinator initially; dedicated outreach coordinator when justified; programme owners retain responsibility for their commitments. |\n| Documentation & knowledge systems | Photography, recordings, archives, contributor information, consent and permissions records, publication files and programme learning. | Named programme owner, supported by communications, editors and commissioned documentation staff. |\n| Hospitality | Guest arrangements, accommodation, catering and visitor support. | Existing Hospitality Team. |\n| Heart & Wellness | Care and belonging throughout the year; programme to be developed together. | Named coordinator with allocated time, accountable to the Director; all leads share responsibility for their teams. |\n| Finance & administration | Budgets, agreements, invoicing, payments, records and reporting inputs. | Programme owners and existing Accounts Team; authorised entity representatives approve commitments. |\n\nA shared planning meeting resolves competing demands on staff, spaces and equipment. Every request includes an owner, brief, deadline and budget.\n\n## Shared resources during Kani Fest\n\nThe Festival Producer coordinates delivery with a named Technical & Infrastructure Lead. Maintain one equipment allocation and movement plan covering venues, operators, crew, setup and changeovers. Venue/session leads confirm readiness; programme changes go through the Programme Coordinator. Shared staff time and specialist crew are budgeted explicitly. Volunteers support assigned tasks under supervision. See [Kani Fest](#kani-fest).\n\nSee [Heart & Wellness](#heart-wellness) for the developing approach to care and belonging across Kani.\n", "platforms": "## Purpose\n\nDevelop recognisable programmes through which Kani’s ethos and pillars take shape. A platform can continue across years while contributors, themes and editions change.\n\nAn original initiative can begin as a small experiment and become a continuing platform if its value, audience, workload and funding justify it.\n\n“Platforms & Original Initiatives” is the working title for this layer. Ownership and usage of particular works, brands and commissioned outputs are addressed through the relevant contributor agreements.\n\n## Platform overview\n\n| Platform | Relationship to the pillars | Creative lead |\n|---|---|---|\n| Kani Fest | Primarily Creative Experiences; draws on research, learning and partnerships. | Festival Curator with proposed 6–9 month paid track fellows; Festival Producer leads delivery alongside the Curator. |\n| Kani Publications | Works across all three pillars; publishes research, creative practice and appropriate commissioned work. | Editor / Publishing Lead on retainer or project appointment initially. |\n| Kani Curated Marketplaces | Primarily Creative Experiences; connects craft and material research with public exchange and maker livelihoods. | Marketplace Curator appointed for a series or edition. |\n| Future original initiatives | Assigned according to their purpose; may connect several pillars. | Named project lead appointed when the initiative is approved. |\n\nEach platform page includes a structure chart and a responsibilities table. The charts distinguish project reporting from shared support; retained and project appointments are not additional permanent Year 1 hires. [Outreach & Community Engagement](#outreach) supports participation across all platforms. Shared [Heart & Wellness](#heart-wellness) supports team morale, listening and practical care across their working teams.\n\nThe Director oversees platform development and resource planning with council guidance. There is no separate permanent “IP creation” department in Year 1.\n\nThe [Heart & Wellness](#heart-wellness) section will be developed together; its activity programme remains open.\n", "kani-fest": "## Purpose\n\nAn interdisciplinary festival with a fresh curatorial proposition for each edition.\n\n## Festival working map\n\n**Drag the map background to move around. Use + / − to zoom, or Reset view to see the whole map. Select any box to read its explainer.** The map describes responsibilities; roles can be combined when time, skills and funding allow.\n\n```kani-map\n[Festival team]\n- Director of Creative Practice | Scope, resources and appointments\n  - Festival Curator | Artistic direction · edition appointment\n    - Arts & New Media Curator | 6–9 month paid fellowship\n    - Sound & Music Curator | 6–9 month paid fellowship\n    - Craft & Material Practices Curator | 6–9 month paid fellowship\n    - Film & Moving Image Curator | 6–9 month paid fellowship\n    - Assistant curators & programme collaborators | Assigned to their respective tracks\n  - Festival Producer | Delivery · fixed-term appointment\n    - Programme Coordinator | Timetable and changes\n    - Technical & Infrastructure Lead | Shared equipment, operators and crew\n    - Venue / Session Leads | Readiness and visitor flow\n    - Artist & Guest Liaison | Requirements, travel and guest coordination\n    - Volunteer Coordinator | Recruitment, induction, rota and welfare\n      - Volunteer teams | Rota and support through the coordinator\n[Shared resources]\n- Funding & Partnerships | Funding relationships and reporting\n- Finance | Budget records and payments\n- Communications | Programme and audience information\n- Community Outreach | Relationships, participation and access\n- Documentation | Consent, archives and learning\n- Hospitality | Stays, food and guest support\n- Programme continuity | Handover between editions\n- Heart & Wellness | Care and belonging · section in development\n- Shared Spaces & Equipment | Venues, equipment and resource availability\n[Collaborations]\n- Festival Curator <> Festival Producer | Collaboration and shared support\n- Shared resources <> Festival Curator | Collaboration and shared support\n- Shared resources <> Festival Producer | Collaboration and shared support\n```\n\nThe Festival Curator and Producer work alongside one another. Assistant curators work through their assigned track curator. Permanent staff retain their reporting lines in [Full Structure](#full-structure). Each volunteer follows one assigned venue or task lead during a shift; the Volunteer Coordinator owns the rota and support.\n\n## Programming flow\n\nProgramme ideas move through a joint creative and resource review before entering one confirmed timetable. Select a step for details.\n\n```kani-flow\n- Curators develop proposals | Content, contributors and requirements\n- Programme & resource review | Festival Curator + Festival Producer\n- Confirmed schedule | Maintained by Programme Coordinator\n```\n\n```kani-details\n| Role / step | Responsibilities and purpose | Working relationships / arrangement |\n|---|---|---|\n| Curators develop proposals | Track curators develop programme ideas with practitioners and communities. Each proposal includes its purpose, participants, space, duration, budget, equipment and support needs. | Festival Curator brings the proposals into a coherent edition and identifies cross-track collaborations. |\n| Programme & resource review | Festival Curator and Producer jointly review artistic coherence and deliverability, with technical and venue inputs. Check parallel sessions, shared equipment, crew, setup and changeovers before confirming the programme. | Curator owns artistic decisions within the brief; Producer owns delivery planning. Unresolved scope or resource conflicts go to the Director; financial authority remains unchanged. |\n| Confirmed schedule | Programme Coordinator maintains and distributes one confirmed timetable. Changes are checked with the relevant leads, logged and communicated to contributors, venue teams and communications. | A living schedule with named owners for sessions and resource movements. Curator and Producer review changes affecting programme or delivery. |\n```\n\n## Volunteer pathway\n\nParticipation can grow with interest, readiness and available opportunities. Progression is optional; sustained responsibilities need a defined, funded role. Select a step for details.\n\n```kani-flow\n- Event volunteer | Defined tasks and supported shifts\n- Returning volunteer / team captain | Bounded responsibility and training\n- Paid associate / fellowship opportunity | Funded role with a clear brief\n```\n\n```kani-details\n| Role / step | Responsibilities and purpose | Working relationships / arrangement |\n|---|---|---|\n| Event volunteer | Join an edition with agreed tasks, induction, availability and a named shift lead. Use a debrief to identify interests, learning needs and whether continued involvement is wanted. | An entry point based on interest and availability; continuing participation is optional. |\n| Returning volunteer / team captain | Take on a clearly bounded coordination or peer-support responsibility, with training, a named staff contact and agreed time commitment. Help brief teams and pass concerns to the Volunteer Coordinator. | Available where readiness and programme needs align. Sustained year-round work should be scoped and budgeted as a paid role rather than assumed to be voluntary. |\n| Paid associate / fellowship opportunity | Apply for a funded role with a defined brief, supervision, learning outcomes, time commitment and compensation. Prior volunteering can inform readiness but is not a requirement or guarantee of selection. | Depends on available funding and roles, interest and a transparent selection process. Curatorial fellowships and delivery associates may have different responsibilities. |\n```\n\n## Curatorial fellowships\n\nThe proposed track roles are **6–9 month paid curatorial fellowships**, covering research, local relationships, programme development, delivery, reflection and handover. The Festival Curator mentors and brings the fellows’ work together. Agree each fellowship’s fee, time commitment, programme budget, learning outcomes and production support before recruitment.\n\nThe four tracks are a working framework. Combine them where appropriate, and allocate poetry, theatre, talks and interdisciplinary projects explicitly within the brief. Each cross-track project has one named owner and a clear budget.\n\n## Roles and responsibilities\n\nDetailed responsibilities, working relationships and appointment arrangements for each part of the map.\n\n### Festival team\n\n| Role / step | Responsibilities and purpose | Working relationships / arrangement |\n|---|---|---|\n| Director of Creative Practice | Agrees the edition brief, budget, scope and appointments. Resolves resource conflicts and reviews delivery with the two festival leads. Financial commitments remain with authorised entity representatives. | Continuing role. Festival Curator and Festival Producer are accountable to the Director for their respective responsibilities. |\n| Festival Curator | Shapes the edition’s artistic proposition and integrates the track programmes. Guides curators, makes artistic selections within the agreed brief and budget, and reviews the programme with the Producer before commitments are made. | Edition appointment covering development, delivery and close-out. Works alongside the Festival Producer; reports to the Director. |\n| Arts & New Media Curator | Develops arts and new-media projects, researches practitioners and supports collaborations. Supplies briefs, budgets and technical requirements to the festival planning team. | Proposed 6–9 month paid curatorial fellowship. Artistic direction from the Festival Curator; delivery coordinated with the Producer. |\n| Sound & Music Curator | Develops sound and music programming, including local artist relationships. Agrees performance requirements and collaborates with the technical lead on soundchecks, shared equipment and changeovers. | Proposed 6–9 month paid curatorial fellowship, reporting artistically to the Festival Curator. |\n| Craft & Material Practices Curator | Develops hands-on workshops, making and material enquiry with practitioners and community partners. Plans materials, participant capacity, facilitation and access with the delivery team. | Proposed 6–9 month paid curatorial fellowship, reporting artistically to the Festival Curator. |\n| Film & Moving Image Curator | Develops screenings, moving-image work and related conversations. Coordinates permissions, screening formats and presentation requirements with the delivery team. | Proposed 6–9 month paid curatorial fellowship, reporting artistically to the Festival Curator. |\n| Assistant curators & programme collaborators | Support research, contributor communication and track preparation. Each collaborator has a named track curator and a defined brief; delivery requirements go through the Producer’s team. | Assigned to the respective track, according to workload and funding. The map groups them for readability; they are not a separate curatorial authority. |\n| Festival Producer | Owns the delivery plan, production budget tracking, vendors, crew and on-site coordination. Tests the programme against spaces, equipment, staffing and changeover time. Resolves operational clashes or escalates them to the Director. | Fixed-term, project-funded appointment spanning planning, delivery and close-out. Works alongside the Festival Curator and reports to the Director. |\n| Programme Coordinator | Maintains the single confirmed timetable, session briefs, contacts and change log. Checks changes with the Curator and Producer and communicates them to venue leads, contributors and communications. | Edition delivery role accountable to the Producer. Maintains the schedule; artistic selections remain with the Festival Curator. May be combined with another coordination role only where capacity allows. |\n| Technical & Infrastructure Lead | Owns the equipment inventory and allocation plan, technical requirements, operators, crew, setup and changeovers. Plans who moves shared speakers or other equipment, when they move, and when checks are completed. Flags clashes before sessions are confirmed. | Named edition lead under the Producer, working with shared Operations / Production and AV staff plus funded technical crew. Volunteers support assigned tasks under supervision. |\n| Venue / Session Leads | Check each space is ready, brief the session team, coordinate starts and changeovers, and manage visitor flow. Escalate technical issues to the technical lead and programme changes to the Programme Coordinator. | Named lead for each active venue or session, accountable to the Producer. Each volunteer has one assigned task lead per shift; experienced team captains may support this work. |\n| Artist & Guest Liaison | Collects contributor requirements, communicates arrival and session information, and coordinates travel and hospitality arrangements. Passes technical needs to the technical lead and schedule changes to the Programme Coordinator. | Edition delivery role under the Producer, working with curators and the shared Hospitality team. |\n| Volunteer Coordinator | Recruits and inducts volunteers, agrees availability and tasks, maintains the rota and supports welfare, briefings and debriefs. Assigns each person to a named shift lead and maintains a route for concerns or changes. | Edition role under the Producer. Coordinates with year-round outreach staff for continuing participation; shift tasks are directed by the assigned venue or task lead. |\n| Volunteer teams | Support agreed tasks such as welcome desks, workshop assistance, visitor guidance, documentation support and supervised setup. Receive induction, clear shifts, breaks and a named lead. Specialist technical tasks remain with qualified crew. | Volunteer Coordinator handles rota and support. The assigned venue or task lead directs each shift. Responsibilities and available support are agreed in advance. |\n\n### Shared resources\n\n| Role / step | Responsibilities and purpose | Working relationships / arrangement |\n|---|---|---|\n| Funding & Partnerships | Develops grant, sponsor and partner relationships; coordinates reporting with programme and finance inputs. Confirms partner commitments with the authorised leads. | Shared support allocated to the edition. Works with the Director, Curator and Producer; existing reporting lines remain. |\n| Finance | Tracks the edition budget, invoices, payments and reporting records with the Producer and programme owners. Financial approvals follow the relevant entity’s delegated authority. | Existing Accounts Team with allocated edition time; programme appointments do not create new approval powers. |\n| Communications | Turns confirmed programme information into announcements, audience information, registration communications and signage. Coordinates changes with the Programme Coordinator and outreach team. | Shared Design & Communications team with additional edition support where funded. |\n| Community Outreach | Builds local relationships, listens to participation needs and supports invitations, access and follow-up. Works with curators on co-development and with the Volunteer Coordinator on continuing involvement. | Year 1 Programme & Operations Coordinator with Director support; dedicated outreach role when justified. Outreach and volunteer management have distinct duties. |\n| Documentation | Plans consent, credits, photography, recordings, archives and the edition review. Gathers learning from fellows, contributors, crew and volunteers and prepares material for possible publication. | Named documentation owner, supported by communications and commissioned contributors. Publishing decisions remain with the publishing lead. |\n| Hospitality | Coordinates accommodation, food, arrivals and guest support using the requirements gathered by Artist & Guest Liaison. Confirms arrangements against the edition budget and capacity. | Existing shared Hospitality Team, with one agreed contact for the festival. |\n| Programme continuity | Keeps relationships, records, calendar coordination and learning connected between editions. Holds the handover from the festival team and helps plan the next cycle. | Programme & Operations Coordinator in Year 1; Creative Experiences Programme Manager at scale. Edition duties need allocated time and additional funded support. |\n| Heart & Wellness | Supports care and belonging across the year. Its activities and programme will be developed together. Notices workload pressures and helps agree practical support, rest and routes for raising concerns. Supports fellows, staff, crew and volunteers; works with the Producer and Volunteer Coordinator on the team’s experience. | Shared responsibility with a named coordinator and allocated time, accountable to the Director. May start within an existing role where capacity allows. Every lead retains responsibility for respectful working and realistic workloads; provide an alternative contact if a concern involves the usual lead. |\n| Shared Spaces & Equipment | Makes shared venues, sound, projection, lighting and other equipment available through one resource plan. Agrees bookings, operators, setup, movement and changeovers with festival delivery leads. | Shared Operations / Production and AV capacity, coordinated with the Festival Producer and Technical & Infrastructure Lead. Existing staff retain their permanent reporting lines. |\n\n## Working arrangements\n\nBegin preparation sufficiently early for the 6–9 month fellowship cycle. The Director agrees scope, resources and staffing with the Festival Curator and Producer. Appointments and fees depend on an approved edition budget; these are proposed roles, not announced vacancies.\n\nIn Year 1, the permanent Programme & Operations Coordinator provides continuity alongside a project-funded Producer, fellows and delivery team. At scale, the Creative Experiences Programme Manager provides that continuity. The festival team uses shared infrastructure with allocated staff time and additional crew where required.\n\nEach edition budgets for outreach, documentation, evaluation and handover. Agree who follows up with contributors, volunteers and partners. See [Outreach & Community Engagement](#outreach), [Shared Infrastructure](#infrastructure) and [Open Decisions](#open-decisions).\n", "publications": "## Purpose\n\nA proposed year-round publishing imprint with its own editorial identity, serving work across all three pillars. “Kani Publications” is a working name.\n\n## Structure chart\n\n```kani-chart\n- Director of Creative Practice | Agrees publishing plan, resources and appointments\n  - Editor / Publishing Lead | Editorial direction and quality\n    - Authors & contributors | Develop the work with the editor\n    - Editorial specialists | Review, copy editing and translation as commissioned\n```\n\n**Chart key:** Solid connections show editorial project direction and accountability. Contributors and commissioned specialists work to an agreed editorial brief; they are not permanent staff reports. The editor owns editorial quality within the approved brief and budget.\n\n> **Shared support — collaboration, not extra reporting lines:** Programme coordination; Design & Communications; community engagement and outreach; Fundraising & Partnerships; Accounts; designated sales and distribution support; Heart & Wellness. These roles support the editorial plan and retain their existing reporting lines.\n\n## Roles and responsibilities\n\n| Role | Responsibilities and decisions | Working arrangement |\n|---|---|---|\n| Director of Creative Practice | Agrees publishing priorities, resources and appointments with the editor; resolves resource conflicts and connects publishing to the wider programme. | Continuing role. |\n| Editor / Publishing Lead | Shapes the editorial programme, develops manuscripts with authors, coordinates review and checks sources, credit and permissions. Owns editorial quality within the agreed brief and budget. | Retained or appointed per title or series initially. |\n| Authors & contributors | Develop original work, supply accurate source and credit information, respond to editorial feedback and agree permissions for their contribution. | Commissioned or agreed contributor arrangements per publication. |\n| Editorial specialists | Provide subject review, language editing and translation according to the title’s needs and editorial standards. | Commissioned specialists coordinated by the editor. |\n| Programme coordination | Maintains schedules, contributor coordination, budget tracking and delivery follow-up; connects publishing with originating programmes. | Permanent coordinator initially, with additional project support where required. |\n| Design & Communications | Develops publication design and works with illustrators and production suppliers; coordinates promotion, launches and audience communication. The editor reviews editorial presentation. | Shared design capacity plus commissioned design, illustration or production support as needed. |\n| Community engagement and outreach | Builds contributor and community relationships, supports accessible participation and helps publications reach intended readers. Credit, consent and editorial permissions remain with the editor and contributors. | Year 1 coordinator and relevant programme owners; dedicated outreach role when justified. |\n| Fundraising & Partnerships | Develops appropriate publishing partnerships, grants and sponsorship proposals with the editor and Director. | Retained or shared fundraising support. |\n| Sales and distribution support | Plans routes to readers, handles orders and stock, and coordinates free or paid distribution and launch availability. | A named project responsibility; the initial allocation remains to be agreed. |\n| Accounts | Supports production-cost records, invoices, payments, sales receipts and financial reporting. | Existing Accounts Team; commitments approved by authorised entity representatives. |\n| Heart & Wellness | Supports welcome, team check-ins, recognition, listening and practical care across the programme. Flags workload pressures and supports follow-through with the relevant leads. | Shared named coordinator with allocated time, accountable to the Director. Every lead retains responsibility for respectful working and realistic workloads. |\n\n## Publishing formats\n\n- Research papers, essays, field notes and working publications.\n- Residency journals, artists’ books and experimental editions.\n- Exhibition catalogues and festival readers.\n- Craft documentation, oral histories and community-authored publications.\n- Learning resources, toolkits and field guides.\n- Zines, digital editions, audio publications and interactive formats.\n- Commissioned or partner-supported publications with an agreed editorial brief.\n\n## Working arrangements\n\nStart with a small, funded editorial programme. The number and type of Year 1 titles remain to be agreed. Programme owners contribute subject knowledge and originating material; the Editor / Publishing Lead holds editorial responsibility.\n\nEvery title has a named editorial owner, intended readership, format, budget and distribution plan. Decide whether it will be free, sold, commissioned or supported by a partner. Publishing income does not remove its research or cultural purpose.\n\nBudget explicitly for editing, design, review, translation, production, outreach and distribution as relevant. Increase retained editorial time only as the funded programme grows; these functions do not require separate permanent hires at the outset.\n\nThe [Heart & Wellness](#heart-wellness) section will be developed together; its activity programme remains open.\n", "marketplaces": "## Purpose\n\nA recurring public programme bringing selected artisans and makers together with demonstrations, workshops and cultural activity.\n\n**Working ambition:** Every two months, for 3–4 days, subject to staffing, funding and participant demand. Confirm the Year 1 frequency separately.\n\n## Structure chart\n\n```kani-chart\n- Director of Creative Practice | Overall resources and accountability\n  - Programme owner | Director in Year 1; Creative Experiences Programme Manager at scale\n    - Marketplace Curator | Theme, participant selection and accompanying programme\n    - Delivery coordinator | Edition schedule, participant logistics and follow-up\n```\n\n**Chart key:** Solid connections show programme and project accountability. In Year 1 the Director also holds programme ownership; that box is a responsibility, not another hire. The permanent Programme & Operations Coordinator initially coordinates delivery. The curator owns selection; delivery coordination does not override that remit.\n\n> **Shared support — collaboration, not extra reporting lines:** Operations / Production and AV; outreach; Design & Communications; Fundraising & Partnerships; Commercial & Bookings; Accounts; Hospitality; Heart & Wellness. These teams retain their existing reporting lines.\n\n## Roles and responsibilities\n\n| Role | Responsibilities and decisions | Working arrangement |\n|---|---|---|\n| Director of Creative Practice | Agrees the series scope, resources and appointments; retains overall accountability. Holds programme ownership in Year 1. | Continuing role. |\n| Creative Experiences Programme Manager | At scale, owns the calendar, coordination and continuity of the marketplace platform; agrees handovers and tracks delivery. | Growth-stage role reporting to the Director; not an additional Year 1 hire. |\n| Programme owner | Holds the platform calendar, scope, budget coordination and delivery handovers. | Director in Year 1; Creative Experiences Programme Manager at scale. A responsibility rather than an additional Year 1 hire. |\n| Marketplace Curator | Selects participants, develops themes and shapes demonstrations, workshops and cultural activities. Works with local practitioners and relevant council advisors. | Series or edition appointment. Creative selection rests with this role within the agreed brief. |\n| Delivery coordinator | Coordinates schedules, participant requirements, logistics and follow-up, escalating resource issues to the programme owner. | Programme & Operations Coordinator in Year 1; allocated or project-funded coordination as the platform grows. |\n| Operations / Production and AV | Plans layout, stalls, equipment, installation and technical support, with named on-site responsibilities. | Allocated team capacity and technical specialists. |\n| Community engagement and outreach | Builds sustained artisan and maker relationships, supports applications and access, and gathers participant feedback. Makes opportunities understandable; does not replace curatorial selection. | Year 1 coordinator with Director support; dedicated outreach role when justified. |\n| Design & Communications | Handles promotion, audience invitations, participant stories and programme information with consent and accurate credits. | Shared communications capacity. |\n| Fundraising & Partnerships | Develops appropriate sponsors and institutional partnerships with the programme owner. | Retained or shared fundraising support. |\n| Commercial & Bookings | Administers stall bookings and fees after curatorial selection, using approved terms. | Named responsibility within Year 1 coordination; dedicated bookings role as justified. |\n| Accounts | Supports fee receipts, invoices, payments and financial records. | Existing Accounts Team. |\n| Hospitality | Supports guest arrangements, food and visitor services against the edition plan. | Existing Hospitality Team. |\n| Heart & Wellness | Supports welcome, team check-ins, recognition, listening and practical care across the programme. Flags workload pressures and supports follow-through with the relevant leads. | Shared named coordinator with allocated time, accountable to the Director. Every lead retains responsibility for respectful working and realistic workloads. |\n\n## Working arrangements\n\nProgramme ownership sits within Creative Experiences. The Director holds that responsibility initially; the Creative Experiences Programme Manager provides continuity at scale. The Marketplace Curator can be appointed across a series, with delivery supported by the permanent team and additional project appointments when needed.\n\nConfirm frequency, stall arrangements, selection criteria, staffing, outreach and budget before committing to a Year 1 series. Fees and booking administration follow selection; they do not determine curatorial acceptance.\n\nAfter each edition, review participant experience, access, attendance, costs and maker feedback. Record what changes for the next edition and who maintains the relationships between events.\n\nThe [Heart & Wellness](#heart-wellness) section will be developed together; its activity programme remains open.\n", "new-initiatives": "Use a simple progression:\n\n1. Identify an enquiry, need or opportunity connected to the ethos and pillars.\n\n2. Define a small pilot with a named owner, audience, budget and outcome. Build outreach into its development: listen to intended participants, identify access needs and agree who maintains the relationships afterwards.\n\n3. Deliver and document the pilot.\n\n4. Review value, local relevance, participation, workload and financial viability.\n\n5. Repeat or revise where justified.\n\n6. Establish a continuing platform only when there is a clear reason and capacity to sustain it.\n", "learnings-feedback": "## A place to listen and learn\n\n**Status: Feedback collection is ongoing.** This section holds programme experience, questions and suggestions separately from the manifesto. The structure below is provisional and can develop as feedback comes in.\n\n## Kani Fest · Edition 1\n\nThe first edition ran for 15 days, with parallel workshops, talks and musical performances. Around 20 volunteers supported different tasks.\n\nThe notes below come from the organiser’s account shared during development of this document. They are initial observations, not a completed evaluation or a consensus from participants. Further feedback may add to or change them.\n\n| Area | Initial observation | Questions to explore |\n|---|---|---|\n| Programming | Programming and parallel workshops and talks worked well. | What felt valuable to participants and contributors? Where did sessions overlap productively or compete? |\n| Making and craft | Hands-on and craft workshops attracted strong interest. | Which formats, timings and facilitation worked best? What support or materials were missing? |\n| Sound and music | Local musicians drew the largest crowds. | What made these gatherings accessible and engaging? How did artists experience them? |\n| Timing | Around seven mornings were quiet across the 15-day edition. | Was this connected to timing, communication, audience availability or programme format? |\n| Infrastructure | Shared equipment moved between spaces, and infrastructure was understaffed. | Where did clashes or delays occur? What crew, equipment and changeover time would have helped? |\n| Production | There was no Festival Producer, and production capacity was weak. | Which responsibilities lacked an owner or adequate time? What needs dedicated coordination? |\n| Volunteers | Volunteers supported varied tasks; some are interested in greater responsibility across the year. | What felt supported or difficult? Who wants to stay involved, and in what capacity? |\n\n### Perspectives still to gather\n\nInvite feedback from participants, local communities, artists, workshop facilitators, volunteers, curators, production and technical crew, hospitality, partners and the organising team. Ask what worked, what was difficult and what they would change.\n\n## A simple feedback structure\n\nUse one record for each distinct observation or suggestion. Keep the original feedback separate from our interpretation and any decision.\n\n| Field | What to record |\n|---|---|\n| Programme / edition / date | Where and when the experience took place. |\n| Perspective and source | Participant, contributor, volunteer, team or partner; how the feedback was collected. |\n| Observation or feedback | What the person experienced, in their own words where appropriate. |\n| Theme | Programme, access, communication, timing, infrastructure, volunteer experience, hospitality, Heart & Wellness or another relevant theme. |\n| Question or suggestion | What needs understanding, testing or changing. |\n| Status | Collected, exploring, agreed for action or deferred. |\n| Response and follow-up | What was decided, why, who will follow up and when it will be reviewed. |\n\n### Working statuses\n\n| Status | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Collected | Recorded; not yet reviewed with the relevant people. |\n| Exploring | More perspectives, detail or a small test are needed. |\n| Agreed for action | A response has been agreed, with an owner and review point. |\n| Deferred | Kept for later, with the reason recorded. |\n\n## Feedback record\n\nThe initial Edition 1 notes above are the starting record. Further participant and team feedback is still being collected; no individual submissions or agreed actions have been entered here yet.\n\nUse **Edit this chapter** to add reviewed notes. This page is a shared record, not a submission form. The collection method and final feedback format remain open for discussion. Keep personal concerns and identifiable sensitive feedback out of the public record; publish agreed or anonymised summaries where appropriate.\n\nAgreed changes to the manifesto should also be recorded in [Version history](#version-history), with the relevant pages updated together. Unresolved structural questions belong in [Open decisions](#open-decisions).\n", "open-decisions": "This edition incorporates the agreed drafting changes. The following questions still need collective agreement before they become operating commitments.\n\n- [ ] Review the ethos wording, three pillar titles and activity menus with the group.\n- [ ] Compare [Council Option 1](#council) and [Option 2 — Participatory Council](#council-option-2), including authority, representation, selection and participation support. Neither model is adopted.\n- [ ] For Option 1, review the seven advisory areas and identify one or two members per area, with one-year appointments and annual review.\n- [ ] For Option 1, agree the council voting procedure: the proposal is one vote per advisory area, a quorum of five areas and four affirmative area votes. Resolve vacancies, disagreements within an area, conflicts and recusals before adoption.\n- [ ] Confirm “Platforms & Original Initiatives” as the layer title and confirm working platform names, including the publishing imprint.\n- [ ] Choose a realistic Year 1 calendar, including festival scope, marketplace frequency and publication commitments and editorial focus.\n- [ ] Confirm outreach priorities, initial community and institutional relationships, allocated staff time and participation-support budgets.\n- [ ] Confirm Year 1 retainer and project-support budgets alongside the three permanent hires; agree platform staffing against actual programmes.\n- [ ] Agree Kani Fest duration and timetable; confirm the Producer, technical lead, shared-resource plan and delivery budget.\n- [ ] Agree curatorial fellowship fees, time commitments, mentoring, selection process and programme support before recruitment.\n- [ ] Confirm volunteer support, team-captain scopes and any funded year-round associate opportunities.\n- [ ] Name the Heart & Wellness coordinator, allocate time and agree support and alternative routes for concerns.\n- [ ] Confirm entity responsibilities and delegated financial authority.\n- [ ] Confirm how publishing sales and distribution will be administered initially.\n\nCollect programme and presentation feedback in [Learnings & Feedback](#learnings-feedback). A suggestion is not an agreed operating decision.\n", "version-history": "## Current edition\n\n**v0.6 · Revised draft — For discussion**  \n**Published:** 4 November 2026\n\nThis edition adds [Governance Option 2 and a comparison](#council-option-2) alongside the existing council proposal. Option 2 explores five advisors, five ex officio curators, two community and two youth representatives, with Heart as a shared foundation. Neither governance option is adopted; decision procedures and representation arrangements remain open.\n\nOption 1 and Option 2 are alternative governance models, not manifesto edition numbers. The organisation remains Kani Home; the document is Kani Manifesto.\n\n## Editions and changes\n\n| Edition | What changed | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| v0.6 · Revised draft · 4 November 2026 | Added a participatory council option and side-by-side comparison; retained Option 1 and its reference decision rights; identified decisions required before adoption. | Explore a more inclusive model without prematurely replacing the earlier proposal. |\n| v0.5 · Revised draft · 2 November 2026 | Three high-level dotted connections without numbered relationship controls; programming and volunteer flows with clickable detail instead of repeated visible tables. | Make the map and pathways easier to read while retaining the detailed responsibilities. |\n| v0.4 · Revised draft · 2 November 2026 | Draggable festival map with shared resources and numbered dotted collaboration links; separate programming and volunteer flows; Heart & Wellness above shared resources with content to be developed; Learnings & Feedback immediately before Review & Editing. | Make working relationships easier to explore and keep developing content easy to find. |\n| v0.3 · Revised draft · 2 November 2026 | Unified expandable festival map with shared resources; year-round Heart & Wellness activities; detailed roles retained; new Learnings & Feedback chapter with collection marked ongoing. Internal analogy and retrospective material removed from manifesto chapters. | Present the proposed organisation clearly while giving experience and evolving feedback their own place. |\n| v0.2 · Revised draft · 2 November 2026 | Clickable Kani Fest working map and role explainers; paid curatorial fellowship model; named delivery and infrastructure responsibilities; programming workflow; supported volunteer pathway; shared Heart & Wellness responsibility; aligned staffing, outreach and decision pages. | Build on first-edition experience and make responsibility, shared-resource planning and routes for participation clear. |\n| v0.1 · Alpha · 2 November 2026 | “An evolving manifesto”; clearer transdisciplinary approach; outreach section and responsibilities across the structure; one-year council appointments with one or two members per area; Sound & Music title; charts and responsibilities for all three platforms; simpler status and review notes. | Prepare a coherent presentation edition and make relationships, responsibilities and the evolution of thinking easier to follow. |\n| Initial website · 1 November 2026 | The first online working document, before numbered editions. | Establish an editable, shared source. |\n\n[Read the preserved v0.6 Revised draft edition](https://bhaumikdebanshu.github.io/KaniManifesto/edition-v0.6.html).\n\n[Read the preserved v0.5 Revised draft edition](https://bhaumikdebanshu.github.io/KaniManifesto/edition-v0.5.html).\n\n[Read the preserved v0.4 Revised draft edition](https://bhaumikdebanshu.github.io/KaniManifesto/edition-v0.4.html).\n\n[Read the preserved v0.3 Revised draft edition](https://bhaumikdebanshu.github.io/KaniManifesto/edition-v0.3.html).\n\n[Read the preserved v0.2 Revised draft edition](https://bhaumikdebanshu.github.io/KaniManifesto/edition-v0.2.html).\n\n[Read the preserved v0.1 Alpha edition](https://bhaumikdebanshu.github.io/KaniManifesto/edition-v0.1.html).\n\n[Read the initial website snapshot](https://bhaumikdebanshu.github.io/KaniManifesto/edition-initial.html). Its content is also preserved in the [original repository commit](https://github.com/bhaumikdebanshu/KaniManifesto/tree/14fac9b564864435f37587fa36fc6e175b9512d5).\n\nSnapshots stay as they were published. The main website carries the latest edition.\n\n## Simple versioning\n\n| Version | When to use it |\n|---|---|\n| v0.1 · Alpha | This first presentation edition. |\n| v0.2 · Revised draft | Earlier revision of the Kani Fest model. |\n| v0.3 · Revised draft | Earlier revision: unified map and separate learning and feedback. |\n| v0.4 · Revised draft | Earlier revision: draggable collaboration map and revised navigation. |\n| v0.5 · Revised draft | Earlier revision: simpler collaboration links and less repetition. |\n| v0.6 · Revised draft | Current revision: two governance options for comparison. |\n| v1.0 · Agreed baseline | When the group agrees a structure to begin working with. It can still evolve. |\n\nDo not change the version for every typo. Increment it when the thinking, responsibilities or structure meaningfully changes. Record what changed, why and what remains unresolved; preserve the previous edition before publishing the next.\n\n## Learning and feedback\n\nProgramme observations and feedback now have a dedicated [Learnings & Feedback](#learnings-feedback) chapter. Collection is ongoing, and its structure remains provisional. Version history records changes made to the manifesto; feedback can remain under discussion without becoming an agreed change.\n\nKeep unresolved operating questions in [Open Decisions](#open-decisions). Check related chapters before publishing an agreed revision; see the [Document review checklist](#document-plan).\n", "document-plan": "## Review one complete edition\n\nReview the manifesto, programme framework, advisory arrangements, team and platforms together. This prevents a change to one role from leaving contradictory responsibilities elsewhere.\n\n## Consistency checklist\n\n| When this changes | Also review |\n|---|---|\n| Ethos or pillars | Home, Programme Framework, pillar chapters, platform descriptions and open decisions. |\n| Advisory areas, appointments or voting | Council, Decision rights, overview text and open decisions. |\n| Outreach responsibilities | Outreach section, Year 1, Full Structure, Growth, shared capabilities and all platform role tables. |\n| Heart & Wellness | Home, Year 1, Full Structure, Growth, shared capabilities, outreach, platform support tables and Decision rights. |\n| Platform staffing or reporting | Platform chart, its responsibilities table, Year 1 support, Full Structure and Decision rights. |\n| Programme observations or feedback | Learnings & Feedback, unresolved decisions, and any agreed changes to the relevant manifesto chapters. |\n| A substantive agreed revision | Version label, version history, previous-edition snapshot, navigation, Markdown export and print view. |\n\nCharts distinguish reporting and project responsibility from shared support. Platform leads and advisors are not additional permanent hires in Year 1.\n\n## Review and publish\n\nCollect proposed edits, review them as a batch, then publish one coherent edition. Keep feedback in [Learnings & Feedback](#learnings-feedback), distinguishing collected observations from agreed actions. Preserve the presentation edition before incorporating feedback so readers can follow how the thinking changed.\n\nSee [Version history](#version-history) for the current edition and revision approach.\n", "editing": "## Edit a chapter\n\n1. Open the chapter you want to change.\n2. Click **Edit this chapter** in the top bar. It opens the corresponding Markdown file on GitHub.\n3. Sign in to GitHub and make your changes. Headings use `##`; tables use rows separated by vertical bars.\n4. Use GitHub’s Preview tab to check the text and table formatting.\n5. Select **Commit changes**, add a short description, and save to the publishing branch if you have permission.\n6. Wait for the Pages deployment to complete, then refresh this website.\n\nThe editor link appears once the site is hosted on GitHub Pages, or when the repository is set in `site.json`.\n\n## Let someone else contribute\n\nShare the website link for reading. Give selected collaborators repository write access if they should edit directly. Others can propose changes through GitHub pull requests. This is repository editing, not simultaneous Google Docs-style editing.\n\n## Tables and checklists\n\nThe site displays tables and checklists from the Markdown files. Make changes in the source file through **Edit this chapter**. Checkboxes on the reading page are intentionally read-only; use `- [x]` in the source to mark a decision complete.\n\n## Download or print\n\n**Download Markdown** combines the current chapters into a single editable document. **Print all** prepares every chapter for printing or saving to PDF.\n\n## One working source\n\nAfter publishing, the Markdown files in this GitHub repository are the website’s working source. The earlier ChatGPT Page and Obsidian folder do not automatically synchronise with it.\n\n## Visibility\n\nThe free public-repository setup makes the site and its source public. The site requests that search engines do not index it, but this is not access control. Anyone with the address can read it.\n\n## Versions and charts\n\nUse [Version history](#version-history) for substantive changes. The main website is the current working edition; the linked snapshots preserve earlier editions and should not be edited. Use **Print all** on a snapshot to save that edition as a PDF.\n\nPlatform charts are written as indented bullet lists inside a `kani-chart` code block in each platform’s Markdown file. Indentation defines the project hierarchy. Kani Fest uses one `kani-map` block with three labelled sections: `[Festival team]`, `[Shared resources]` and `[Collaborations]`. Indented bullets define the team hierarchy; flat bullets define shared resources. Collaboration entries use `- Role A <> Role B | Relationship label`, matching box names exactly. The programming and volunteer flows use separate `kani-flow` blocks below the map. Each box title matches the first cell of its explanation table exactly; the next cells supply the explainer text. The programming and volunteer explanations are in `kani-details` blocks directly beneath their flows. These tables supply the clickable explainers and print output without repeating on the reading page. Edit those table cells to change an explainer. Keep map collaboration links at the level of the two festival leads and shared resources. Update the chart and table together when a role or relationship changes. Shared support stays separate from reporting lines.\n\nBefore publishing a substantive new edition, update the version and date in `site.json`, the history page and the preserved snapshot together. Follow the [Document review checklist](#document-plan).\n\nProgramme observations and feedback belong in [Learnings & Feedback](#learnings-feedback). Its collection format is provisional; use reviewed summaries when updating this public record.\n"}};
